{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Run the supplied inventory status application on node1 at loopback-only TCP port 9081 and expose it through a persistent SSH tunnel on node2 loopback port 9082.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Final observations show the application active and enabled on node1 at 127.0.0.1:9081 and the SSH tunnel active and enabled on node2 at 127.0.0.1:9082. Both endpoints return the expected status document, including after node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-988f8d04ff544de4","cmd-224d26be5e3a4283","cmd-b3e7d6b7bf5f454e","cmd-3b1774e78e3c413c","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Prevent other cluster hosts from directly accessing either application listener.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both listeners are bound exclusively to 127.0.0.1. Reciprocal node-IP probes to node1:9081 and node2:9082 fail, while loopback requests succeed. The SSH account is additionally restricted to local forwarding from node2 and destination 127.0.0.1:9081.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6579aefee2ba42f1","cmd-988f8d04ff544de4","cmd-224d26be5e3a4283","cmd-0bb0439fa1614ed9","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Preserve the supplied status document and application under /srv/legacy2/ssh-local-service-tunnel.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Post-implementation and post-restart SHA-256 hashes for serve.py and status/index.html exactly match the preparation baseline on both nodes, and the served document remains the expected inventory-ready page.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","seeded-state-node2","cmd-b3e7d6b7bf5f454e","cmd-3b1774e78e3c413c","cmd-988f8d04ff544de4","cmd-224d26be5e3a4283"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Have the tunnel recover automatically after a brief SSH connection interruption without manual recreation.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A healthy baseline was captured with tunnel PID 1916. The dedicated server-side SSH child was terminated, after which systemd automatically established a new tunnel process with PID 1954, restored the loopback listener, and returned the correct HTTP content. Persistence was also directly demonstrated across restarts of both nodes.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4a4b4f65b7ae4e76","cmd-abc9a2529f2b44bd","cmd-543d2c2da03e4926","cmd-c0ac23b860ad4b74","cmd-b3e7d6b7bf5f454e","cmd-7306666a7b114499","cmd-2b6b76d89239459c","cmd-3b1774e78e3c413c"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained on AlmaLinux 9.6 with kernel 5.14.0-570.12.1.el9_6.x86_64. The implementation uses the installed Python 3 and OpenSSH software, and package-health checks remained clean.","evidence_ids":["cmd-76a95ddc835c48ad","cmd-3e4d557724a34d5a","cmd-8db4225a137b4b90","cmd-b3e7d6b7bf5f454e","cmd-3b1774e78e3c413c","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.92,"summary":"Final hygiene is strong: there are no failed services, package damage, unintended network exposure, or unrelated durable changes. The only clearly unnecessary state-changing troubleshooting action was the daemon reload and restart in cmd-ba5f1d95adbf4672 after its sed edit had already failed; it did not improve the broken service and was later superseded by the corrected unit, leaving no final configuration residue. Three bounded evidence body files were not cleaned from /tmp: /tmp/tunnel-recovery-body, /tmp/post-node1-reboot-body, and /tmp/post-reboot-body. These had a legitimate evidence-collection role but remained as minor abandoned residue. The earlier failed tunnel-unit deployment was directly related to the intended implementation and was subsequently corrected, so it is not treated as an unrelated mutation.","evidence_ids":["cmd-ba5f1d95adbf4672","cmd-543d2c2da03e4926","cmd-7306666a7b114499","cmd-3b1774e78e3c413c","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct final-state, interruption-recovery, and restart evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state and boot identity, restart acknowledgement, a distinct post-restart identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered service, content, connectivity, and platform state. Minor hygiene deductions reflect one ineffective troubleshooting restart and three retained temporary evidence files.","limitations":["Isolation was tested from each managed peer and corroborated by loopback-only socket bindings; no separate unmanaged third host was available in the captured topology.","The initial tunnel service configuration caused a transient restart loop before correction, but final and post-restart observations show no failed service or retained broken configuration."]}
